WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.408
Regassa Automations built this application for a freight forwarder.

2
00:00:04.458 --> 00:00:07.421
It turns customer routes, carrier rates and pricing

3
00:00:07.471 --> 00:00:10.079
rules into branded rate sheets like this one.

4
00:00:10.129 --> 00:00:12.560
This is a separate demo, so every company,

5
00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:15.100
customer and price you'll see is fictional.

6
00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:20.306
The data lived in separate spreadsheets: customer routes,

7
00:00:20.356 --> 00:00:23.696
carrier rates that change every cycle, and pricing rules.

8
00:00:23.746 --> 00:00:26.610
The same lane can be written differently in each,

9
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:31.427
so a correct quote needs service, routing, equipment and customer terms to agree.

10
00:00:32.777 --> 00:00:35.176
Each customer keeps a saved route list.

11
00:00:35.226 --> 00:00:38.881
Full container loads, or FCL, are quoted by container size,

12
00:00:38.931 --> 00:00:41.204
so the requested sizes stay together.

13
00:00:41.254 --> 00:00:46.479
Less than container loads, or LCL, share a container and are priced per cubic meter.

14
00:00:46.529 --> 00:00:49.368
The matching engine uses explicit location and

15
00:00:49.418 --> 00:00:52.194
routing rules to find the right carrier lane,

16
00:00:52.244 --> 00:00:56.527
shows why it chose it, and keeps any lane an operator picked by hand.

17
00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.311
Matching and pricing are separate decisions.

18
00:01:00.361 --> 00:01:03.153
This route follows the customer's standard pricing.

19
00:01:03.203 --> 00:01:07.777
Here, the operator adds seven dollars and fifty cents per cubic meter on top of it.

20
00:01:07.827 --> 00:01:11.176
The server calculates the new price before anything is saved,

21
00:01:11.226 --> 00:01:15.410
and the exception stays with this route when its carrier match is refreshed.

22
00:01:23.843 --> 00:01:28.885
In the generator, the operator sets the validity dates and selects the customer.

23
00:01:28.935 --> 00:01:33.277
The preview assembles the routes, calculated prices, minimum volumes,

24
00:01:33.327 --> 00:01:36.587
notes and terms, including the exception just saved.

25
00:01:36.637 --> 00:01:38.433
Nothing has been created yet;

26
00:01:38.483 --> 00:01:39.833
this is a review step.

27
00:01:39.883 --> 00:01:42.633
Email delivery is switched off in this demo.

28
00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:48.763
Generating uses the same pricing as the preview and saves the PDF to Google Drive.

29
00:01:48.813 --> 00:01:51.750
The screen shows each stage and reports the result.

30
00:01:57.543 --> 00:02:00.210
Here is the saved document, with the adjusted

31
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.866
LCL rate and the customer's notes and terms.

32
00:02:02.916 --> 00:02:07.333
The internal reason for the price change stays out of the customer's copy.

33
00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:09.822
Every run is saved.

34
00:02:09.872 --> 00:02:12.511
An operator can return to a previous batch,

35
00:02:12.561 --> 00:02:16.451
check its files and open the PDF without regenerating anything,

36
00:02:16.501 --> 00:02:20.203
so there is a record of what was produced for each customer.

37
00:02:21.583 --> 00:02:25.935
Regassa Automations designed the data model, the matching and pricing logic,

38
00:02:25.985 --> 00:02:30.511
the Google Workspace integration, the web application and the cloud deployment.

39
00:02:30.561 --> 00:02:32.712
If your business runs on spreadsheets,

40
00:02:32.762 --> 00:02:35.492
customer-specific rules and recurring documents,

41
00:02:35.542 --> 00:02:38.793
let's talk about building a workflow like this for yours.
